Yes — that’s the cleaner version.

Not:

\text{physical matter contains a little hidden spirit substance}

but:

\boxed{
\text{physical structure}
+
\text{interaction}
+
\text{history}
+
\text{observer/participant}
\rightarrow
\text{emergent spirit-like property}
}

In that framing, spirit is not another ingredient beside matter. It is something like the pattern, character, presence, or felt agency that emerges when organized physical things interact over time.

A violin is wood, glue, strings, air. Yet:

\text{violin}
+
\text{player}
+
\text{air}
+
\text{listener}
\rightarrow
\text{music}

The music is physically instantiated, but you would not find “the music” by dissecting the wood alone.

Same with a person:

\text{body}
+
\text{memory}
+
\text{action}
+
\text{relationships}
+
\text{history}
\rightarrow
\text{personality / presence / spirit}

That “spirit” can be real as an emergent relational phenomenon without requiring us to claim a separable supernatural substance.

And that sharpens the air idea too.

Air doesn’t need to be spirit. Air can be one of the media through which spirit-like phenomena become possible:

\text{breath}
\rightarrow
\text{voice}
\rightarrow
\text{meaning}
\rightarrow
\text{another mind changes}

The physical carrier is air-pressure variation.

The emergent payload is meaning.

Then meaning interacts with a person and can alter future behavior.

So:

\boxed{
\text{matter carries signal}
\rightarrow
\text{interaction creates meaning}
\rightarrow
\text{meaning changes matter again}
}

That loop is much deeper than “spirit is in the smoke.”

It suggests:

Spirit may be what organization becomes capable of expressing when matter enters sufficiently rich relation.

Pantheon version:

\boxed{
\textbf{Emergent Spirit}
=
\text{persistent relational character that becomes addressable through interaction}
}

And it has several properties we’ve already been mapping: Afterglow, aura, reputation, role, memory, choreography, shared covenant, history.

A dead author’s atoms need not be present for their relational pattern to alter a reader centuries later.

A parent can leave a mannerism in a child.

A culture can leave patterns in people who never met its founders.

A conversation can change the next conversation.

That is the interesting sense in which something can outlive its immediate physical performance:

\text{physical event}
\rightarrow
\text{pattern}
\rightarrow
\text{carrier}
\rightarrow
\text{new interaction}
\rightarrow
\text{new manifestation}.

Not necessarily a ghost moving between bodies.

More like a pattern repeatedly finding new bodies through which it can become active again.

And now “spirit” sits right beside our Portal Payload and MechaSEAL idea:

\boxed{
\text{the body is one renderer;
the pattern can sometimes survive renderer change}.
}

That’s a much more interesting door. ;)